---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import SocialTool from '../components/SocialTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Resize an Image for LinkedIn, No Upload | SizeTune';
const description =
  "Fit a picture to LinkedIn's profile cover, company logo and shared link image sizes in your browser. Sizes come from LinkedIn's own Help pages, with dates.";
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: resize an image for LinkedIn', '/linkedin-image-resizer/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/linkedin-image-resizer/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Resize an image for LinkedIn" lead="Choose a LinkedIn size, decide how the picture should fit, and download a JPEG. The sizes come from LinkedIn's own Help pages, and nothing is uploaded." />

  <SocialTool client:load platform="linkedin" />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This tool fits images inside your browser, so it will not run while JavaScript is turned off. Enable JavaScript and
      reload to use it. The notes below on LinkedIn's image sizes still apply with any other editor.
    </p>
  </noscript>
  <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl text-center text-base text-slate-700">
    Need the file under a size limit? <a data-testid="to-resizer" class="font-medium text-teal-800 underline underline-offset-4" href="/">Open the KB resizer</a>.
  </p>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>The LinkedIn sizes in this tool</h2>
    <p>
      There are three presets, each taken from a LinkedIn Help page that states the number. The profile cover image
      is recommended at 1584 by 396 pixels, as a JPG or PNG under 8 MB. The logo for a company Page is recommended at
      400 by 400 pixels, with a minimum of 268 by 268, as a PNG or JPEG under 3 MB. An image attached to a post with a
      link uses a 1.91:1 ratio, which is 1,200 by 627 pixels.
    </p>

    <h2>A size we left out on purpose</h2>
    <p>
      A company Page also has a cover image. the sources we found give different numbers for
      it. Rather than offer a number we cannot stand behind, the tool
      leaves it out. If you need it, read the current figure on LinkedIn's Page image specifications help page before
      you upload.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the profile cover is so wide</h2>
    <p>
      The cover image is a thin strip behind your photo, about four times wider than it is tall. A normal photo does not
      have that shape, so something has to give. With crop, the tool keeps a band across the picture, and you choose
      whether it comes from the top, the middle or the bottom. With colour bars, the whole picture is shown small in the
      middle of the strip, which rarely looks good for a cover. A blurred background works better for a wide image you
      want to show whole.
    </p>

    <h2>Logos and transparent backgrounds</h2>
    <p>
      LinkedIn's Page help says a logo with a transparent background is shown on white. This tool saves JPEG, which has
      no transparency, so the bars colour decides what appears behind the logo. Choose white to match LinkedIn, or pick
      the colour of your brand. Keep important details away from the edges of the cover, since LinkedIn's help notes that
      it can be trimmed on some screens, especially in the lower-right.
    </p>

    <h2>Steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Add your picture by dragging it onto the box or choosing it.</li>
      <li>Choose the LinkedIn size from the list. Its source and date are shown beneath it.</li>
      <li>Pick crop, colour bars or blurred background and check the preview.</li>
      <li>Download the JPEG. If you need a smaller file, set a size limit in KB.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about LinkedIn images</h2>
    <h3>Which format does LinkedIn accept for these images?</h3>
    <p>The help pages we read name JPG or PNG for the profile cover and PNG or JPEG for Pages. This tool saves a JPEG.</p>
    <h3>Why is there no profile photo preset?</h3>
    <p>We only list a size when we could read it on a LinkedIn Help page. We did not find one we could verify for the profile photo.</p>
    <h3>How current are the sizes?</h3>
    <p>Each names its source and the date we last read it, and our sizes guide lists the official page. LinkedIn can change its figures, so check that date.</p>
    <h3>Is my picture uploaded to SizeTune?</h3>
    <p>
      No. It is fitted in your browser. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>. For other platforms, try the{' '}
      <a href="/instagram-image-resizer/">Instagram</a> or <a href="/youtube-thumbnail-resizer/">YouTube</a> pages.
    </p>
  </div>
  <RelatedTools path="/linkedin-image-resizer/" />
</Base>
